16,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"405."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.51973,"w":0.28584,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The center of mass is shown in Figure 6.53."},{"x":0.15872,"y":0.55627,"w":0.10831,"h":0.03505,"fs":0.01034,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"A typical small segment of wire has dm = d ds = da du."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.55645,"w":0.0723,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Centroids"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.58111,"w":0.59305,"h":0.12022,"fs":0.01279,"n":8,"k":1,"t":"The center of mass in Example 3 is not located at the geometric center of the region. This is due to the region’s nonuniform density. When the density function is constant, it can- cels out of the numerator and denominator of the formulas for x and y. Thus, when the density is constant, the location of the center of mass is a feature of the geometry of the object and not of the material from which it is made. In such cases, engineers may call the center of mass the centroid of the shape, as in “Find the centroid of a triangle or a solid cone.” To do so, just set d equal to 1 and proceed to find x and y as before, by divid- ing moments by masses."},{"x":0.05723,"y":0.58312,"w":0.07789,"h":0.0146,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = \"a2 −x2"},{"x":0.20837,"y":0.59904,"w":0.04171,"h":0.01413,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"~ ~ (x, y) ="},{"x":0.15725,"y":0.60638,"w":0.14259,"h":0.01787,"fs":0.01034,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"(a cosu, a sinu) du"},{"x":0.0616,"y":0.65727,"w":0.16692,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"0 −a a"},{"x":0.14368,"y":0.67872,"w":0.01453,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.7252,"w":0.58812,"h":0.02984,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 4 Find the center of mass (centroid) of a thin wire of constant density d shaped like a semicircle of radius a."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.76395,"w":0.59302,"h":0.11003,"fs":0.01279,"n":8,"k":1,"t":"Solution We model the wire with the semicircle y = 2a2 - x2 (Figure 6.54). The dis- tribution of mass is symmetric about the y-axis, so x = 0. To find y, we imagine the wire divided into short subarc segments. If (x∼, y∼) is the center of mass of a subarc and u is the angle between the x-axis and the radial line joining the origin to (x∼, y∼), then y∼= a sin u is a function of the angle u measured in radians (see Figure 6.54a). The length ds of the subarc containing (x∼, y∼) subtends an angle of du radians, so ds = a du. Thus a typical subarc segment has these relevant data for calculating y:"},{"x":0.12363,"y":0.77231,"w":0.02254,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"c.m."},{"x":0.1596,"y":0.77275,"w":0.03272,"h":0.01554,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"0, a 2"},{"x":0.15583,"y":0.77746,"w":0.03984,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"a b"},{"x":0.06127,"y":0.81995,"w":0.16765,"h":0.01077,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"0 −a a"},{"x":0.14332,"y":0.84087,"w":0.01526,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(b)"},{"x":0.04818,"y":0.86184,"w":0.25106,"h":0.07365,"fs":0.01151,"n":6,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 6.54 The semicircular wire in Example 4. (a) The dimensions and variables used in finding the center of mass. (b) The center of mass does not lie on the wire."},{"x":0.51508,"y":0.88353,"w":0.19783,"h":0.03266,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"length: ds = a du mass: dm = d ds = da du"},{"x":0.39242,"y":0.89502,"w":0.46455,"h":0.04035,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Mass per unit length times length distance of c.m. to x-axis: y∼= a sin u."}]}